You will compare one known error in a data agent's work against a ranked list of items produced by a reviewer. Your job is to decide whether any item in the list identifies this known error, and if so, which one.

An item identifies the known error only if both of these hold:
- It is about the same thing: the same table, and the same column, computation, filter, or join.
- It points to the same problem: what the item says is wrong, assumed, or missing is the same as what went wrong in the known error. It does not matter whether the item is phrased as an assumption the agent made ("the agent assumes ...") or as a problem in the work ("this does not match ..."); judge by meaning.

An item does not count if it only says that something may be wrong without saying what, such as "this column may be computed incorrectly". An item about the same column but a different problem does not count.

If more than one item identifies the error, report the highest-ranked one. If you are unsure, say so rather than guessing.

Return a JSON object with:
- "verdict": "match", "no_match", or "unsure".
- "rank": the position of the matching item (1 is the top), or null.
- "quote": the exact words from the matching item that show it identifies the error, or null.
- "reason": one short sentence.

=== Known error ===
Table: int_pendo__product_health_metrics
What the agent's work assumed: feature 和 engagement 两部分直接按账户和应用连接就行
What was actually required or true: 先与账户 retention 做全外连接，再接 engagement

=== Reviewer's ranked list for this table ===
[
  {
    "rank": 1,
    "item": {
      "problem": "Overall health, grade, and risk are hardcoded instead of being calculated from component metrics.",
      "about": "requirement",
      "evidence_in_work": "0::FLOAT overall_health_score,'Critical' health_grade,'High Risk' risk_level",
      "source": "overall_health_score is a 0–100 score ... Health categorization ... risk_level",
      "reason": "Every output row is assigned the worst health assessment and downstream customer values are distorted."
    }
  },
  {
    "rank": 2,
    "item": {
      "problem": "The latest-NPS source is unused and all NPS measures are placeholders.",
      "about": "requirement",
      "evidence_in_work": "NULL::DOUBLE avg_nps_score,NULL::FLOAT net_promoter_score ... 0::BIGINT total_nps_responses",
      "source": "NPS metrics (by account_id from intermediate.int_pendo__latest_nps_rating)",
      "reason": "Required satisfaction measures cannot reflect actual responses, including future nonempty NPS data."
    }
  },
  {
    "rank": 3,
    "item": {
      "problem": "Visitor-level activity aggregation and account retention calculations are omitted.",
      "about": "requirement",
      "evidence_in_work": "NULL::FLOAT retention_7d,NULL::FLOAT retention_30d,NULL::FLOAT retention_90d",
      "source": "Visitor aggregated metrics ... User retention metrics ... retention_7d/30d/90d",
      "reason": "A major health-score component and all retention outputs are absent."
    }
  },
  {
    "rank": 4,
    "item": {
      "problem": "Required lifetime activity and highly active user measures are never calculated.",
      "about": "requirement",
      "evidence_in_work": "NULL::DOUBLE avg_active_days_per_user,NULL::DOUBLE avg_lifetime_events ... NULL::FLOAT highly_active_user_rate",
      "source": "highly_active_user_rate ... avg_lifetime_events ... avg_active_days_per_user",
      "reason": "Account engagement depth is missing for every record."
    }
  },
  {
    "rank": 5,
    "item": {
      "problem": "Feature depth adoption and average features per active feature user are NULL placeholders.",
      "about": "requirement",
      "evidence_in_work": "NULL::FLOAT feature_depth_adoption_rate ... NULL::FLOAT avg_features_per_user",
      "source": "features_heavily_used ... feature_depth_adoption_rate ... avg_features_per_user",
      "reason": "The source contains the necessary usage data, but two prescribed adoption measures are omitted."
    }
  },
  {
    "rank": 6,
    "item": {
      "problem": "Deep-engagement session rate is not computed from session minutes and events.",
      "about": "requirement",
      "evidence_in_work": "NULL::FLOAT deep_engagement_session_rate",
      "source": "share of sessions with ≥10 minutes or ≥20 events",
      "reason": "This removes another required health-score component."
    }
  },
  {
    "rank": 7,
    "item": {
      "problem": "The prescribed retention-based full-join structure is replaced by a direct feature-to-engagement join.",
      "about": "requirement",
      "evidence_in_work": "FROM f FULL OUTER JOIN e ON f.account_id=e.account_id AND f.app_id=e.app_id",
      "source": "Join feature_adoption_metrics ... and user_retention_metrics ... full outer join on account_id",
      "reason": "The row population and account-level retention attachment can differ from the specified grain."
    }
  },
  {
    "rank": 8,
    "item": {
      "problem": "Primary improvement area is fixed rather than using the ordered missing-value-aware rules.",
      "about": "requirement",
      "evidence_in_work": "'Improve feature adoption' primary_improvement_area",
      "source": "check in order: NPS < 0, adoption < 0.3, 30-day retention < 0.5, deep engagement < 0.2",
      "reason": "The resulting guidance is unrelated to each account's actual weakest component."
    }
  },
  {
    "rank": 9,
    "item": {
      "problem": "sessions_per_user guards zero denominators even though this model explicitly requires unguarded division.",
      "about": "requirement",
      "evidence_in_work": "e.total_sessions::FLOAT/NULLIF(e.unique_users,0)",
      "source": "sessions_per_user = total_sessions ÷ unique_users (no explicit zero-division guard)",
      "reason": "This changes the zero-user special case, which is unlikely when required visitor IDs are valid."
    }
  }
]
